    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Before the 4th century BCE Macedon was a small kingdom centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aigai</a>, briefly a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> under Amyntas I and Alexander I, who fought on the Persian side at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> in 479 BCE.</li><li>Philip II (359–336 BCE) rebuilt the army around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">sarissa</a>-armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>, took the mines of Crenides and Amphipolis, and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> at the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE.</li><li>Elected hegemon of the League of Corinth in 337 BCE, Philip was assassinated by his bodyguard Pausanias of Orestis at his daughter's wedding feast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aigai</a> in 336 BCE, and Alexander succeeded him.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Alexander I of Macedon was at first refused entry to the foot race at the Olympic Games because only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> could compete; he produced an Argead genealogy tracing his line to Temenus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argos">Argos</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellanodikai">Hellanodikai</a> let him run.</p></aside>
    </section>

