    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/perdiccas.jpg" alt="Perdiccas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philip_III_Arrhidaios_Babylon_mint_struck_under_Perdikkas_circa_323_320_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG coins, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> record the legend that the Argead kings of Macedon descended from Temenus, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argos">Argos</a>, and so from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>. Contradictory traditions name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a> I or Caranus as the dynasty's founder, with five or eight kings before Amyntas I, and little is known of the kingdom before Amyntas's reign (547–498 BCE). Robert Malcolm Errington suggests that one of the earliest Argead kings made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aigai</a>, modern Vergina, the capital in the mid-7th century BCE.</p><p>The kingdom first lay along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haliacmon">Haliacmon</a> and Loudias rivers north of Mount Olympus and along the coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermaic-gulf">Thermaic Gulf</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>, Paeonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>. It expanded into Emathia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pieria">Pieria</a>, Bottiaea, Almopia, Mygdonia, Crestonia and Bisaltia, inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a> among others, and into Eordaea and Upper Macedonia, home of the Greek tribes of the Lyncestae, Orestae and Elimiotae. A year after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the Persian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megabazus">Megabazus</a> persuaded Amyntas I to submit as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. Macedonia was never made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a>, but it supplied troops: Alexander I supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> in 480–479 BCE, and Macedonians fought on the Persian side at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a>. The Persian withdrawal from Europe soon afterwards ended Achaemenid control.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/silver"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silver.jpg" alt="Silver" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_crystal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alchemist-hp, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Young Macedonians hunted and fought as a by-product of a transhumant life herding goats and sheep; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">horse breeding</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raising were common, and some farmed, often with irrigation, land reclamation and horticulture supported by the state. The economy and the royal finances rested mainly on logging and on mining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, iron, gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, and working these materials into finished goods encouraged the growth of towns and a gradual shift away from the rustic Macedonian way of life in the 5th century BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> valued the region's silver, gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and pitch enough to found Amphipolis on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Strymon</a> in 437/436 BCE to supply its navy.</p><p>Silver coinage began under Alexander I to pay royal expenses; Archelaus I raised its silver content and added copper coins to promote commerce, and minting grew greatly under Philip II after the seizure of the Pangaion Hills. Revenue also came from the produce of arable land, timber from the forests and harbour taxes on imports and exports; tariffs existed from at least the reign of Amyntas III, and Callistratus of Aphidnae helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a> III double the annual customs profit from 20 to 40 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>. State mines, groves, farmland and forests were exploited by the king or leased and granted to the hetairoi and philoi. Society was headed by the king and a martial aristocracy second only to him in power and privilege, and Macedonia seems to have lacked the widespread use of slaves seen in contemporary Greek states.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sarissa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarissa.jpg" alt="Sarissa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">Sarissa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Makedonische_phalanx.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">F. Mitchell, United States Military Academy, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The army was divided between the companion cavalry, the hetairoi, and the foot companions, the pezhetairoi, who may have existed since the reign of Alexander I, with allied troops, foreign levies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. The cavalry, noblemen in muscled cuirasses, became renowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>; the infantry were poorly trained shepherds and farmers. Nicholas Viktor Sekunda puts the army at 10,000 infantry and 600 cavalry when Philip II acceded in 359 BCE.</p><p>Philip II made this levy a professional force. He introduced the Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> armed with the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">sarissa</a> pike, gave the infantry peltai shields, helmets, greaves and cuirasses or kotthybos stomach bands, formed the elite hypaspistai and the royal bodyguards, the somatophylakes, adopted the Scythian flying-wedge cavalry formation, hired Cretan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and Thracian, Paeonian and Illyrian javelin throwers and slingers, and employed engineers to build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a> and bolt-firing artillery. The mines of Crenides, refounded as Philippi in 356 BCE, paid for a permanent standing army and the kingdom's first small navy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a>. This army beat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> and Paeonians, took Amphipolis, Pydna and Potidaea in 357–356 BCE, crushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onomarchus">Onomarchus</a> at the Battle of Crocus Field in 352 BCE, destroyed Olynthos in 348 BCE and, after failed sieges of Perinthus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantion">Byzantion</a> in 340 BCE, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> at the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/basileus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basileus.jpg" alt="Basileus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/basileus">Basileus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antiochos_I_Tetradrachm_620447.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the head of the state stood the king, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basileus">basileus</a>. Before Philip II the monarchy is the only institution the sources attest; from his reign the king was assisted by royal pages conscripted from aristocratic households, bodyguards, companions, friends and an assembly that included members of the army. How far these shared authority is uncertain: council members spoke freely and could press the king, and the assembly apparently judged treason, as when Alexander prosecuted three alleged conspirators in his father's murder. The king was chief judge, supreme commander and high priest; Philip II performed daily sacrifices and may have borrowed Persian institutions such as a royal secretary, a royal archive and a seated throne.</p><p>Direct father-to-son succession broke after the assassination of Orestes in 396 BCE, allegedly by his regent Aeropus II; four kings reigned in the chaos of 399–393 BCE before Amyntas III killed Pausanias and claimed the crown. Macedonians traditionally practised monogamy, but Philip II married seven wives to bind the Upper Macedonian aristocracy and his allies, among them Phila of Elimeia, the Illyrian Audata, Philinna of Larissa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympias">Olympias</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, who bore Alexander. Philip was elected archon of the Thessalian League, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a>'s two seats on the Amphictyonic Council, and in 337 BCE became hegemon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> autokrator of the League of Corinth, of which his kingdom was officially not a member.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="Aristotle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Lysippos, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Though on the periphery of Archaic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classical-greece">Classical Greece</a>, the Argead court drew the Greek world to it. Archelaus I was crowned at Olympia and Delphi for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> racing and received the painter Zeuxis, the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a>, the poets Choerilus, Timotheus and Agathon, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> moved to Macedonia, tutored the young Alexander and served Philip II as a diplomat. The royal tombs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Vergina</a>, with murals of Hades abducting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a>, golden coffins and arms, and the bronze Derveni Krater show an elite that imported Greek artistic traditions while keeping older funerary rites.</p><p>Philip II's reign, with his son's, saw the demise of Classical Greece and the birth of Hellenistic civilization. His League of Corinth, uniting the major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> under a Macedonian hegemon for a war presented as punishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and the liberation of the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, was the model <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a> revived a century later. His army, coinage and standing force were the instruments with which Alexander overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> and culture to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. The kingdom also left a lasting argument over whether the Macedonians were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>: scholars such as Hatzopoulos and N. G. L. Hammond hold that the distinction was political, monarchy against city-state democracy, contrived after the League of Corinth of 337 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/olympias"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olympias.jpg" alt="Olympias" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/olympias">Olympias</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_olympias_mus_theski.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fotogeniss, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Argead kingdom did not fall in 336 BCE; it became the core of an empire. Philip II's last years were troubled at home. His marriage to Cleopatra Eurydice, niece of the general Attalus, and talk at the wedding feast of new heirs infuriated Alexander and his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympias">Olympias</a>, who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> until Alexander was recalled to Pella. To reconcile with Olympias he married their daughter Cleopatra to her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-epirus">Alexander I of Epirus</a>, and at the wedding feast in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aigai</a> in 336 BCE he was assassinated by his bodyguard Pausanias of Orestis.</p><p>Alexander III was at once proclaimed king by an assembly of the army and leading aristocrats, and buried his father at Aigai. The League of Corinth revolted at the news, was quelled by force and diplomacy, and elected Alexander hegemon for the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. In 335 BCE he subdued the Triballi and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a>, then razed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>, killing 6,000 and taking 30,000 prisoners, and the next year crossed to Asia. The Argead line ended in the wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-arrhidaeus">Philip III Arrhidaeus</a> was executed by Olympias in 317 BCE and the boy Alexander IV put to death by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> in 311/310 BCE, and the Antipatrid and Antigonid dynasties held the throne until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> abolished the monarchy in 168 BCE. The diffusion of Greek culture that Philip's kingdom made possible was, in Errington's judgement, a precondition for Rome's expansion into the East and the basis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Macedon under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argead-dynasty">Argead dynasty</a> grew from a small realm at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aigai</a>, briefly a Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and long a pawn between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, into the master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>: Philip II's reformed army and diplomacy won Chaeronea and the League of Corinth, and his assassination in 336 BCE handed the kingdom to Alexander, who made it the core of an empire.</em></p>

